{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~6–15 EUR), especially beyond ~day 5. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a local peak in history, the model keeps prices elevated and declines too slowly. Introduce faster mean reversion when the last 10–20d slope is negative or volatility is rising.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following sharp run-ups in the history window, the model extrapolates strength; realized outcomes revert lower. Cap upside continuation and pull forecasts toward a medium-term mean (e.g., 20–30d average) when recent returns are strongly positive.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: the overestimation widens with forecast horizon (largest errors in weeks 2–4). Use a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: model path is too smooth and too high during drawdowns; realized series shows deeper troughs. When recent drawdown exceeds a threshold, increase downside sensitivity (steepen projected declines)."
  ]
}
